  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "David Crossland entered the travel trade at 16 after leaving Burnley Grammar School with three O-levels. A decade later he borrowed to buy a two-shop Lancashire agency, then added outlets steadily and began organising his own package holidays. His instinct for affordable mass-market travel drove Airtours into charter flying, long-haul destinations and a nationwide retail network.\n\nCrossland floated Airtours on the London Stock Exchange and pursued acquisitions across Europe and North America. The group added airlines, hotels and cruise operations, at its peak serving millions of customers and carrying a multi-billion-pound market value. He renamed the business MyTravel and stepped down after returning during a financial crisis, ending a 31-year tenure that helped reshape Britain’s package-holiday market.",
    "html": "<p>David Crossland entered the travel trade at 16 after leaving Burnley Grammar School with three O-levels. A decade later he borrowed to buy a two-shop Lancashire agency, then added outlets steadily and began organising his own package holidays. His instinct for affordable mass-market travel drove Airtours into charter flying, long-haul destinations and a nationwide retail network.</p>\r\n<p>Crossland floated Airtours on the London Stock Exchange and pursued acquisitions across Europe and North America. The group added airlines, hotels and cruise operations, at its peak serving millions of customers and carrying a multi-billion-pound market value. He renamed the business MyTravel and stepped down after returning during a financial crisis, ending a 31-year tenure that helped reshape Britain’s package-holiday market.</p>\r\n",
